WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.370
For many, Italy has become the biggest threat
to the entire Eurozone.

00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:04.370
Why?

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:15.589
We’ll tell you all about it in this video.

00:00:15.589 --> 00:00:20.350
The Cradle of the West, pasta, pizza, and
popular coffee-making techniques like the

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:23.260
cappuccino, ristretto, and espresso.

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:27.300
But in addition to all that, Italy has become
quite a headache for Europe’s leaders.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:37.930
No wonder, we’re talking about a giant that
could turn into a true Titanic.

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:42.350
An economic and financial crisis that could
make Greece’s crisis look like child’s

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:43.350
play.

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:46.400
See, Italy currently has the eighth largest
economy in the world...

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.870
But this country isn’t growing, its banking
system has loads of problems and its government

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:52.260
is hugely indebted.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:57.180
In fact, Italy’s public debt already surpasses
130% of its GDP, which means that even though

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:01.820
it has the fourth largest economy in the European
Union after Germany, the United Kingdom, and

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:04.500
France, it also has the largest public debt
stock.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:08.050
Which amounts to more than 2.5 billion dollars.

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:13.330
The Italian public debt, folks, is twice the
size of the entire Mexican economy and almost

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:15.190
4 times that of the Swiss economy.

00:01:15.190 --> 00:01:22.760
And that, my friends, is a lot, and I mean
a lot, of money.

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:29.990
But the problem doesn’t even begin to end
there.

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:33.360
The Italian economy is hardly growing, in
fact, it hasn’t managed to get back to its

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:38.780
pre-2008 crisis levels; unemployment surpasses
10%, and youth unemployment lies at around

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:40.630
40%.

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:47.800
To all this we need to add the fact that,
year after year, public debt continues to

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:52.140
rise, and it won’t stop growing; the country
has a very big population-aging problem and,

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:56.010
above all, the European Central Bank, which
in recent years has been Italy’s largest

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:59.830
public debt purchaser, could soon stop:

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:05.229
("ECB says its massive bond-buying program
will likely end in December”

00:02:05.229 --> 00:02:09.140
All of that said, we can easily understand
news like this:

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:13.510
("Italy is pushing Europe to the brink of
another economic crisis".

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:17.330
("The Italian crisis that is making the EU
tremble worsens”

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:19.459
Now, some of you may be wondering:

00:02:19.459 --> 00:02:22.860
Hey Simon, the problems you're talking about
aren’t precisely new, so, why is Italy in

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:23.860
the spotlight?

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:24.860
Why has it gone back to the front pages?

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:29.020
Why the hell should we suddenly be so worried?

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:32.640
Well, listen up.

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:38.370
(NEW GOVERNMENT: A STEP BACK)

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:42.480
After months of uncertainty, a new government
finally gained power in June 2018.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:47.450
And we’re not talking about just any government.

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:51.020
Led by the new Prime Minister Giuseppe Conte,
we’re talking about a government formed

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:56.959
by the Eurosceptic and anti-system movement
Five Stars, and by La Liga, an extremely far-right

00:02:56.959 --> 00:02:57.959
wing party.

00:02:57.959 --> 00:03:01.890
This new coalition arrived with a very clear
idea in mind: Italy’s sovereignty would

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:05.239
again be in Rome and not in Brussels…

00:03:05.239 --> 00:03:09.170
Which in most cases wouldn’t mean anything
except a higher expenditure.

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:16.130
Well, the fact is that, as you know, the countries
that belong to the Eurozone, that is, those

00:03:16.130 --> 00:03:20.459
that share the same currency, agree on certain
goals with the European authorities, as well

00:03:20.459 --> 00:03:25.630
as on some maximum public deficit levels,
and on a path that these deficits have to

00:03:25.630 --> 00:03:27.160
follow over the years.

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:32.620
All of this is done to maintain their stability
and solvency and thus reduce the risk of crisis.

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:37.209
Well, the new Italian government doesn’t
like this one bit.

00:03:37.209 --> 00:03:38.209
- Deficit path?

00:03:38.209 --> 00:03:39.209
Come on.

00:03:39.209 --> 00:03:40.209
Now you’re going to tell me how to spend
or not spend my money?

00:03:40.209 --> 00:03:41.209
Well you seem to have a very clear idea of
what we should do.

00:03:41.209 --> 00:03:44.780
This is, more or less, what the new government
must’ve thought... because one of the first

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:49.209
things they did was present a budget proposal
in September for a public deficit that is

00:03:49.209 --> 00:03:52.540
substantially larger than the one they committed
to in Brussels.

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:55.890
And that's when the earthquake happened.

00:03:55.890 --> 00:04:02.041
( Italy defies the European Union and increases
their deficit to 2.4% of the GDP.

00:04:02.041 --> 00:04:03.459
- A new government wanting to spend more?

00:04:03.459 --> 00:04:05.099
But Italy has a public debt problem.

00:04:05.099 --> 00:04:07.739
And now they want to ask for a higher debt?

00:04:07.739 --> 00:04:08.849
Seeing is believing.

00:04:08.849 --> 00:04:13.870
Because, folks, 2.4% may sound low, but if
we take the fact that the Italian economy

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:18.949
is forecasted to not grow more than 1% in
the upcoming years into account, then things

00:04:18.949 --> 00:04:20.039
change…

00:04:20.039 --> 00:04:25.330
Because that 2.4% now means that with every
year that passes, Italy will become less solvent--that

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:29.120
is, it’ll be even less able to pay its debts.

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:33.400
Well, do you know what came of this dialogue
between the new Eurosceptic government and

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:34.400
Brussels?

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:36.130
Check it out.

00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:40.199
Since Giuseppe Conte’s new government’s
orientation was exposed, the 10-year Italian

00:04:40.199 --> 00:04:43.219
bond’s profitability soared by more than
90%.

00:04:43.219 --> 00:04:47.389
That is, investors now understand that there’s
a very high risk and therefore require a higher

00:04:47.389 --> 00:04:49.590
profitability for lending their money.

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:53.050
Obviously this means that every time that
the Italian government issues debt, it has

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:56.599
to include higher interests.

00:04:56.599 --> 00:05:00.580
This means that up to the year 2020, Italy
will have to face debt maturities of almost

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:05.520
600 billion dollars, an amount that, of course,
it’ll have to request again from the markets,

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:08.879
along with the deficits it has accumulated
until then.

00:05:08.879 --> 00:05:11.389
This amounts to more than 700 billion dollars.

00:05:11.389 --> 00:05:14.800
- These numbers are insane, right?!

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:19.999
Well, if investors ask for more profitability,
interest will be higher, and so will the public

00:05:19.999 --> 00:05:20.999
deficit...

00:05:20.999 --> 00:05:23.180
And if, in addition, the European Central
Bank stops buying debt...

00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:27.089
it could mean even higher interests... to
the point that this spiral could put the Italian

00:05:27.089 --> 00:05:31.589
government on the ropes, and then its banks,
as they own a good part of Italy’s debt.

00:05:31.589 --> 00:05:35.849
Also, it doesn't seem likely that they’ll
be able to withstand a new recession.

00:05:35.849 --> 00:05:39.369
"Italy’s Banks Stumble Into a New Crisis.

00:05:39.369 --> 00:05:41.150
And of course, that’s not all.

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:44.979
Italy isn’t Greece, its huge size could
mean the spread of their financial crisis

00:05:44.979 --> 00:05:46.379
throughout the entire Eurozone.

00:05:46.379 --> 00:05:50.870
That’s why the new Italian government’s
policies have led to so much concern.

00:05:50.870 --> 00:05:54.430
Italy is a time bomb that could explode at
any moment.

00:05:54.430 --> 00:05:58.249
That’s why it doesn’t seem likely that
the European Central Bank will stop funding

00:05:58.249 --> 00:05:59.610
the party.

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:06.029
"The only reason Italy isn’t already rated
junk is because the rating agencies assume

00:06:06.029 --> 00:06:09.759
the ECB will step in to prevent a default.

00:06:09.759 --> 00:06:13.400
In that sense, Italian bonds are actually
German bonds.

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:14.449
John Rubino

00:06:14.449 --> 00:06:17.399
The question is, how long can this situation
last?

00:06:17.399 --> 00:06:20.300
But now that we know what’s happening, the
question that remains is, how did they get

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:22.119
to this point?

00:06:22.119 --> 00:06:25.119
Listen up.

00:06:25.119 --> 00:06:28.569
(THE ORIGIN OF THE PROBLEM)

00:06:28.569 --> 00:06:33.289
Not so long ago, we dedicated a video to how
Italy arrived at this situation.

00:06:33.289 --> 00:06:36.250
We can basically summarize it as a lack of
competitiveness.

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:39.319
For years, Italian politicians really didn’t
care about the economy.

00:06:39.319 --> 00:06:44.240
While the world changed at full speed, Italy
punished companies with extremely high taxes

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:46.499
and lots of regulations of all kinds.

00:06:46.499 --> 00:06:47.819
And that’s not all.

00:06:47.819 --> 00:06:52.110
The state kept hiring more and more officials
and spending money without really caring about

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:53.110
the results.

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:57.169
For example, only 1 in 5 young Italians gets
a university degree, a rate that’s almost

00:06:57.169 --> 00:06:58.559
half the OECD average.

00:06:58.559 --> 00:07:03.060
And of course, if you aren’t educating your
youth and forget about being competitive,

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:04.189
what else can you expect?

00:07:04.189 --> 00:07:09.289
If that weren’t enough, by adopting the
euro, Italian politicians can no longer use

00:07:09.289 --> 00:07:12.139
one of their favorite tricks: devaluing the
currency.

00:07:12.139 --> 00:07:16.229
So this, folks, this is what explains the
dark news that we occasionally see in the

00:07:16.229 --> 00:07:17.229
media.

00:07:17.229 --> 00:07:19.879
As you can see, the situation is very complicated.

00:07:19.879 --> 00:07:23.260
Here at VisualPolitik we’ll try to keep
up with all the events.

00:07:23.260 --> 00:07:26.919
But now it’s your turn: Do you think the
Italian government should break with Brussels?

